Code Review:-

Its using TS where performing GET call and fetching user Data.

1.) userId is used in useEffect but not mentioned in dependency array because of that it will run once on first render.
If userId prop changes then it won't refetch.
It should be like this.
useEffect(() => {
    fetch(`https://api.example.com/user/${userId}`)
}, [userId]);

2.) For user State, type is not defined. Additionally, any is being used that will cause TS type safety concern.
It should be like this.
interface User {
    name: string;
    bio: string;
}

then 
const [user, setUser] = userState<User | null>(null);

3.) Errors are only logged in console. It could be added as UI feedback.
Like for example. 
const [error, setError = userState<string | null>(null); 
......
.catch(error => {
    console.error('Any Meaningful error message', error);
    setError('Any Meaningful error message');
});
In render it can be shown. 
{error ? <p> {error} </p> : user ? (...) : <p> Loading...</p>}

4.) Code should have proper consistent formatting and indentation. 
5.) Hardcoded API url because of that anyone inspecting JS bundle can see this endpoint. 
it should be 
fetch(`${ProcessingInstruction.env.REACT_APP_API_URL}/user/${userId}`)
6.) Error handling for HTTP status codes i.e. 403, 404 or 500that needs to be parsed. 
like this
fetch(...)
    .then(response => {
        if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`Http ${response.status}`);
        return response.json();
    })

Unit Test Review:-

1.) Additional tests should also be included for:- 
    a.) Rendered user :- Mock a successful fetch and check for user.name and user.bio
    b.) Handling error state :- Mock a failed fetch i.e. 404 or 500 and check for error message
    c.) Updates on userId change :- Re-render with new userId and check for new user Data
2.) For fetch call no mocking done because of that a real network request will happen with no control over the response

    It should be used like
    beforeEach(() => {
        jest.spyOn(globalThis, 'fetch').mockResolvedValue({
            json: () => Promise.resolve({ name: 'shashank', bio: 'developer'}),
        } as Response);
    });

3.) Missing async handling because react updates state asynchronously specially after fetch, so use 
findByText for elements that appear after delay. 

like 

const userName = await screen.findByText('shashank');
expect(userName).toBeInTheDocument();